1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,060
 Okay, so now it's time to teach you a bit of complexity about text processing.

2
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:11,800
 Up till now, we've kind of been ignoring the complexity of text processing.

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:17,840
 Everything that I have been doing, most of what I've been doing is in ASCII,

4
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:23,240
 the Latin character set, the character set that, you know, United States, Europe,

5
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:27,040
 lots of Western civilizations use this character set.

6
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:34,160
 And if you go back to the 1950s and 1960s, they, we were happy to have one computer,

7
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:37,440
 and we didn't care what the character set was, as long as what you typed on

8
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:42,360
 the keyboard came out on the printer, the internal representation didn't matter.

9
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:47,280
 And as the 70s and 80s came along, certainly 70s,

10
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:50,800
 we needed some interoperability, and so they standardized that character set.

11
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:52,720
 But they standardized that character set,

12
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:56,360
 certainly in the West, that did not represent anything.

13
00:00:56,360 --> 00:01:02,640
 And so, if you look at this sheet, basically what it's telling you is for

14
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:06,240
 the various characters, there's some non-printing characters,

15
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,960
 white space, non-printing characters, and then here's some printing characters like

16
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:13,840
 the and key, the zero, and then the uppercase characters, and

17
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:19,020
 then the lowercase characters, and there's 128 of these possible values.

18
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:22,920
 And there are nothing even for Spanish or French in here.

19
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:28,080
 And it's also why, by the way, uppercase letters in Latin sort lower than

20
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:31,760
 lowercase letters, and we saw that in some of the string stuff.

21
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:37,960
 And what these do is it maps and says, okay, and

22
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:45,000
 a lowercase a maps to the number integer number 97, which in base 16 is 61 and

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,760
 in octal it's 141, but in binary, this is, it's 8-bit numbers.

24
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:53,160
 And so, these are 8-bits, otherwise known as a byte.

25
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:59,240
 And they're very efficient, like when you buy a disk drive, it's megabytes or

26
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:04,020
 gigabytes or whatever, that's how many of these kind of characters it can store.

27
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:09,240
 But unfortunately, this doesn't work for more complex characters.

28
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:15,800
 You can figure out these numbers inside of Python by using the ord function.

29
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,680
 And so, you say, what is the ordinal or the numeric representation of the uppercase

30
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:23,840
 h, lowercase e, and newline is a character as well.

31
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:27,240
 And so, like 10 is the ordinal position of newline.

32
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:29,040
 And this actually has to do with sorting, so

33
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:33,320
 that e lowercase e is higher than uppercase h.

34
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:38,200
 And that's just because in the simplest of sorts, we just sort them numerically.

35
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:41,320
 So newline, if you go back to the previous little sheet,

36
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:45,600
 newline is this 10 right here, it's that 10, which is a line feed.

37
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:49,080
 And that's a 10, and that's why when we print newline out, we get a 10.

38
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:53,800
 And so again, in the early days when strings were simple,

39
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:56,480
 we just represented them as one byte per character.

40
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:02,120
 But the problem is, is that as we have gotten more complex, and

41
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:05,320
 in today's modern world, it's simply unacceptable to say that the only thing

42
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:08,180
 computers can understand is ASCII.

43
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:13,240
 And so this leads to a very, very, from the simplest of character sets to

44
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:16,680
 a super complex character set called Unicode.

45
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:20,040
 Which basically is billions of characters,

46
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:25,400
 potential billions of characters for every language and every character set.

47
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:29,960
 And because there's so much space in Unicode, it's easy to take very small

48
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:32,600
 variations of characters and give them a space.

49
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:39,880
 It's so large that you can have pretty much any character that you want.

50
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:40,840
 So that's Unicode.

51
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,800
 The problem is, is that if we sent Unicode across the network,

52
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,560
 it would be way too large.

53
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:53,200
 It'd be this UTF32, which instead of being eight bytes per character,

54
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:55,160
 would be four bytes per character.

55
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:58,560
 And so it would take all of the data that we build and

56
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:03,560
 make it four times larger, and it'd be very difficult.

57
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:07,440
 And so what they've come up with is ways to compress this.

58
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:15,440
 And UTF16 is this weird thing, UTF32 is really sort of the full Unicode pretty

59
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:21,800
 much, UTF16 is a subset of Unicode, it's used in some countries.

60
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:27,320
 But the best practice for moving data across the internet or

61
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:32,160
 in a file that you're going to move between computers is what's called UTF8.

62
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:35,760
 And so what happens is, is that UTF32 is fixed length.

63
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:44,640
 ASCII is one byte, UTF16 is two bytes, UTF32 is four bytes.

64
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:50,920
 And UTF8 has dynamic length, meaning that it is one to four bytes.

65
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:54,640
 And if it's only one byte long, it's perfectly compatible with ASCII,

66
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:58,480
 meaning that an ASCII file is also a UTF8.

67
00:04:58,480 --> 00:04:59,760
 And so here's this little sheet, and

68
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:02,360
 it's not critical that you understand this graph too much.

69
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:07,040
 But basically, as time passed, 2000 internets coming, coming, coming,

70
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:10,920
 coming out 2014, pretty much overwhelmingly the documents on

71
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:13,680
 the internet that you might retrieve are UTF8.

72
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:19,800
 Now, so UTF8 is the recommended practice, and it's sort of a compression.

73
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:24,600
 UTF8 can represent all the things UTF32 can represent.

74
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:30,120
 It's just a compression of it with an overlap of ASCII, which is awesome.

75
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:30,920
 It's what you want.

76
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:33,720
 I don't need to talk anymore.

77
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:39,480
 So in Python, we have always had sort of two ways of representing strings.

78
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:44,440
 In Python 2, the normal string was a byte string,

79
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:47,680
 was an ASCII string, was a Latin string.

80
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,560
 And if you wanted to represent Unicode,

81
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:55,560
 there was a separate kind of object that we had, and so you would do that.

82
00:05:55,560 --> 00:06:02,720
 And in Python 3.0 or later, one of the main features of Python 3

83
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,320
 was to make Unicode and string the same.

84
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:10,900
 So that means inside of Python, when you have a string variable, it's a Unicode.

85
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:14,800
 Whereas inside of Python 2, it was a byte variable.

86
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:20,120
 And so now we have this notion separately in Python 2 and

87
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,720
 on Python 3 where we have byte variables.

88
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:28,320
 And so byte variables are, in effect, an array of bytes.

89
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:32,520
 So if there's A, B, C, that means it's three bytes, it's three bytes long.

90
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:35,160
 Whereas a string, a three character string,

91
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:37,800
 might be anywhere from three to 12 bytes long.

92
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:42,440
 So Python 2 had bytes and strings that were the same.

93
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:47,080
 Bites and strings are the same, and Unicode is weird.

94
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:53,560
 And in Python 3, Strings and Unicode are the same.

95
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:56,040
 And bytes are weird, okay?

96
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:58,520
 And so that's what we've got to deal with.

97
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:04,560
 And there'll be times when we get bytes from APIs, when we call things,

98
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:07,880
 we have to then figure out what kind of thing those bytes contain.

99
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:11,240
 Cuz the bytes might contain ASCII, they might contain UTF-8,

100
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:13,440
 they might contain various things.

101
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:16,800
 And so internally, all the strings in Python 3 are Unicode.

102
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:20,880
 Most of the time, if you're inside the program or

103
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:22,880
 reading and writing files, we just work.

104
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:24,800
 And that's why we haven't mentioned it.

105
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:26,520
 But now that we're talking over sockets, and

106
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:29,000
 we're talking to the sort of random world out there,

107
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,320
 we have to be a little more aware of the data we're dealing with.

108
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:38,800
 Now, the good news is 98% of the time, or 95% of the time, it's UTF-8.

109
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:41,960
 Which might also include ASCII, and so it's quite nice.

110
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:44,160
 But we have to be aware of this.

111
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:51,640
 And so if we are going to take data that comes off of the network in the bytes,

112
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:57,320
 then we have to make sure that we interpret it or decode it in the right way.

113
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:01,680
 So that internally, the strings, which are Unicode, are properly represented.

114
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:06,320
 And so that's why when we read data in from a network connection,

115
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:09,520
 like a socket, we have to say, hey, decode it.

116
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:13,520
 Now, there's a couple things going on at that moment of decode.

117
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:19,240
 And so this is where we're doing it, we have to manage this in this code,

118
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:22,120
 where before we send this stuff, we're going to encode it,

119
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:27,200
 which takes a Unicode string and turns it into UTF-8 bytes.

120
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:30,240
 There's actually a parameter here that you could do it different than UTF-8,

121
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:31,800
 but no one ever does.

122
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:34,520
 You might have to for certain situations, but so

123
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:38,640
 that says that we're going to encode this into UTF-8 before we send it.

124
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:42,800
 And then when we get something back, before we print it, we're going to decode it.

125
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:45,320
 And that's how this ends up working out.

126
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:49,540
 And if you look at the documentation, you will see that sometimes it says

127
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:52,080
 it's a string or it's bytes.

128
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:57,880
 And so you take a byte array and you decode it to get a string and

129
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:00,600
 you take a string and encode it to get a byte array.

130
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:03,240
 And so that's what we're doing.

131
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:06,040
 So you can think of the process as this way.

132
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:14,520
 And that is the network has these UTF-8, mostly UTF-8 resources, not ASCII.

133
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:16,080
 If it's ASCII, it's okay.

134
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:18,760
 So you read with the receive.

135
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:23,080
 So this receive here pulls data, well, we have a Unicode string.

136
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:24,720
 Let's start with the send.

137
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:27,400
 So up here, we have a Unicode string.

138
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:28,680
 That's a Unicode string.

139
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:31,760
 Even though there's no special characters in it, no Asian characters or

140
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:34,720
 French characters, that's a Unicode string.

141
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:38,280
 And before we can send it, we have to send it in UTF-8.

142
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:41,400
 If that had Asian characters, it'd be okay.

143
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:44,960
 And that would be set up just right so that the UTF-8 would be right.

144
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:47,760
 So we encode it first, and that's the CMD.

145
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:49,160
 This is now bytes, okay?

146
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:50,840
 CMD is bytes.

147
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:53,760
 And then we actually send the bytes, and that goes across the network.

148
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:57,800
 We get back our thing, and we receive, and we receive into data.

149
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:01,720
 Well, data is bytes, not string, it's bytes.

150
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:03,400
 We can say how big it is.

151
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:05,720
 Function's kind of like a string and it has len,

152
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:11,480
 except that it is one byte per character, which means some of it might be UTF-8.

153
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:13,120
 And then all we have to do is say decode.

154
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:16,400
 Again, you could, if you were dealing with a situation where you weren't expecting

155
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:22,000
 it to typically be UTF-8 or ASCII, you could tell it UTF-16 or something.

156
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:23,280
 And it's more complex.

157
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:27,160
 But the simple thing is you just say, I'm going to clean up my data on the way in.

158
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:28,920
 I'm going to clean it up by running it through decode.

159
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:30,920
 And I'm going to encode stuff on the way out.

160
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:35,400
 And so sockets are the place where this comes into play.

161
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:39,040
 And so you'll see, we'll always do this encode and decode every time we're

162
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:43,640
 sending data kind of outside of Python and inside of Python.

163
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:45,880
 So now that we've talked a little bit about character sets,

164
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:50,160
 we're going to make this even easier so you don't have to use sockets.

165
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:54,000
 We're going to, urllib is a bit of Python code in the library

166
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:55,800
 that does all the socket stuff for you.

167
